  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge holds over colorado today continuing our \nabnormaly warm pattern likely through Sunday. Showers are currently \ndeveloping in the higher terrain. Temperatures 10-20 degrees warmer \nthan average are expected across the lower elevations this weekend. \nThe ridge shifts east Friday to make way for the trough heading in \nfrom the Pacific Northwest providing us with southwest flow aloft, \nbut high temperatures in the mid/upper 80s are still expected for \nthe plains. Denver will once again near the record high Friday of 87 \nset back in 1910. Saturday will \"cool off\" slightly but well above \naverage temperatures are still expected. Winds will increase on \nFriday and Saturday supporting critical fire weather conditions for \nWashington, Elbert, and Lincoln counties.\n\nSunday the weather pattern will finally change, but the overall \nimpacts for our area will be limited. Temperatures will be in the \nupper 70s and low 80s due to slight cooling aloft and precipitation \nchances will increase for our western counties as the moisture from \nRachel and the digging trough clash. There remains some uncertainty \nhow far east the precipitation will reach, but overall model \nconsensus favors the greatest chances in Utah and Western Colorado.\nTuesday night into Wednesday night the trough and low starts to push \ninto our area, but deterministic and ensemble guidance still doesn't \nshow significant indications of rainfall for the area. Likely due to \nthe storms/moisture having to make it over the mountains before they \ncan precipitate in the plains.Rainfall amounts will likely depend on \nhow deep the trough will reach and the exact track. Rain and \nmountain snow showers will be possible. \n\nThere is a lot going on from Wednesday through Friday. The upper \nlevel trough that was over southern California finally makes its way \nacross the Central Rockies, in a weakened state. Depending on its \ntrack, rain and mountain snow showers will be possible on Wednesday. \nOn the heels of this system, an upper level low dives south-\nsouthwest from western Canada possibly into the Central Rockies. \nMany questions remain regarding this system yet, but even colder air \nis expected if it dives far enough south. Roughly 20 to 40 percent \nof the ensemble members show snow for the lower elevations sometime \nWednesday through Friday. Even if precipitation missed the area, \nhigh temperatures are expected to be near or below normal (50s-60s) \nacross northeast Colorado.",
